The AIL and MS AdLib frequency models stay inside their octaves and their table at the lowest notes

Upstream took the rest of this patch its own way, in abba426 ("Prevent possible errors at models and fixed the out-of-range at HMI SOS model"): HMI SOS reads its bend table no further than its last entry, as this patch had it, and the models that convert a tone to an unsigned type assert that the tone is not below zero, which OPL3::noteOn() makes sure of. The two faults left are not below zero; ordinary notes reach them, and this patch still fixes them.

oplModel_ailFreq() adds an octave offset that counts how far below the table the note was, and a tone below 12 can make the octave negative. Upstream now asserts that it is not, so a build with assertions stops at the first low note played with the AIL volume model, and a build without them shifts the negative octave into the block field, which is undefined:

    src/models/model_ail.c:153: uint16_t oplModel_ailFreq(double, uint32_t *): Assertion `octave >= 0' failed.
    src/models/model_ail.c:161:27: runtime error: left shift of negative value -1

The octave is held at zero before the assertion, as oplModel_hmiFreq() holds it, with the comment "Don't wrap everything to top!".

oplModel_msAdLibFreq() adds a half-tone offset that a downward bend makes negative in the lowest octave, and then reads its frequency table at that note:

    src/models/model_msadlib.c:114:12: runtime error: index -1 out of bounds for type 'uint16_t[12]'

The lowest note is as far down as it goes.

Over the tones from 0 to 256, every sixteenth of a tone, the answer changes at 110 tones of AIL, those from 0 to 6.8125, where the sign of the octave stood in the frequency (a tone of 0 answered 0xfeb2, and answers 0x02b2 with this), and at 84 tones of MS AdLib between 0.5625 and 11.9375, where the table was read off its front; every other answer of the eight models is the same, and none of them does anything that the undefined behaviour sanitizer reports. A fuzz target of ADLplug-Next that plays MIDI through the real-time API, with the volume model a saved project can hold, found the first; calling each model over the range of tones found the second.

This is ADLplug-Next's own patch, applied to the submodule by cmake/Patches.cmake when CMake configures, and it goes away once the change is upstream.

diff --git a/src/models/model_ail.c b/src/models/model_ail.c
index 9fff731..92adfe4 100644
--- a/src/models/model_ail.c
+++ b/src/models/model_ail.c
@@ -150,6 +150,9 @@ uint16_t oplModel_ailFreq(double tone, uint32_t *mul_offset)

     octave += octaveOffset;

+    if(octave < 0)
+        octave = 0; /* Don't wrap everything to top! */
+
     assert(octave >= 0);

     while(octave > 7)
diff --git a/src/models/model_msadlib.c b/src/models/model_msadlib.c
index 94cfd8d..7254b33 100644
--- a/src/models/model_msadlib.c
+++ b/src/models/model_msadlib.c
@@ -111,6 +111,10 @@ uint16_t oplModel_msAdLibFreq(double tone, uint32_t *mul_offset)
     }

     note += halfToneoffset;
+
+    if(note < 0)
+        note = 0; /* Don't go below the lowest note! */
+
     freq = s_msAdLibFreqTable[delta][note % 12];
     octave = note / 12;

